# Everything under secrets/ is ignored except the committed example. A
# deny-by-default rule rather than a list of filenames: the Drive service-account
# key arrived as a new file nobody had listed, and it was one `git add .` from
# being committed.
secrets/*
!secrets/secrets.example.toml
.env
# The web client's own env files (`web/.env.local` carries the Supabase
# project URL and publishable key). Same deny-by-default reasoning as
# secrets/ above: a `.env` rule alone does not match `.env.local`.
.env.local
.env*.local
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
dist/
build/
# Agentic engineering workflow harness — kept private (developed toward a
# future plugin). Untracked but present on disk so the local workflow keeps
# running. The full workflow handbook lives in CLAUDE.local.md (also ignored).
.claude/
CLAUDE.local.md
PR_BODY.md
PR_BODY.generated.md
.tdd-branch-cleanup.log
# --- COPYRIGHT: never commit source books or any book-derived content ---
# The source PDFs and every ingested artefact (vault notes, extraction trees,
# envelopes, chunk/tag/artifact/xref records, raw console logs) contain VERBATIM
# copyrighted text. Everything under data/ is ignored by default so book text
# can never be swept in by `git add -A`.
#
# The exceptions below are the audit trail: what was run, what it cost, what it
# measured. They are allow-listed BY FILENAME, never by directory, so a new file
# dropped into an allow-listed folder stays ignored until someone names it here.
#
# The rule for adding one: it may carry counts, hashes, timings, prices, model
# names, source_ids and the operator's own prose. It may NOT carry passage text,
# note bodies, name-page evidence, model answers, or generated papers.
data/**

# Run logs (see "Run logging" in the handbook): the operator's write-up of the
# run, the per-unit event stream, and the throwaway scripts that measured it.
# `console.log` stays out — it is raw stdout, 8 MB of it is progress lines, and
# it echoes prompts.
!data/logs/
!data/logs/**/
!data/logs/**/summary.md
!data/logs/**/run.jsonl
# A run with more than one arm writes one event stream per arm
# (`run.control.jsonl`, `run.styled.jsonl` -- issue #787 slice 05). Same
# record, same reason to keep it; still narrow enough to leave `*-before.jsonl`
# and the other corpus snapshots below ignored.
!data/logs/**/run.*.jsonl
!data/logs/**/*.py
!data/logs/**/*.sh
# `.ps1` for the same reason as `.sh`: this is the box the runs happen on, and
# a run whose script is not committed cannot be repeated (issue #784).
!data/logs/**/*.ps1
# Everything else in a log dir is a corpus snapshot or model output:
# `*-before.jsonl`, `alias_map*.json`, analyses, papers. Those stay ignored.

# What corpus a number was measured on: author, title, year, publisher, ISBN,
# page count, file hash. Bibliography, not text.
!data/source_meta/
!data/source_meta/*.json

# Eval and sweep roll-ups: cost, latency and model wiring per brief. The
# `analyses/` and `papers/` beside them stay ignored.
!data/runs/
!data/runs/**/
!data/runs/**/summary.json
!data/runs/**/worklist.txt

# The coverage and system reports, the prose they are assembled from, the
# scripts that build them, and the rendered .docx and logo a reader needs.
# This directory is the ONE place the by-filename rule above is relaxed to a
# by-directory one (founder ruling 2026-08-06: the reports belong on GitHub),
# so the copyright test moves onto whoever adds a file here.
#
# What may live in data/reports/: the operator's own prose, counts, prices,
# model names, source_ids, chunk_ids, section headings, and Axial's OWN
# generated output — a rendered paper is Axial's writing, not a book's. What
# may NOT: passage text, note bodies, name-page evidence, or any other
# verbatim run of a source. Checked at v2.0 — every block quote in
# axial-report.md is Axial's own prompt, brief, or explanatory note, and the
# appendix papers cite by id and heading, never by quotation.
!data/reports/
!data/reports/**/
!data/reports/**
# Word writes a lock file beside any .docx it has open. By-directory means it
# would otherwise be swept in the moment someone reads the report.
~$*
# Build cruft: Python bytecode cache, and the mermaid-diagrams-spliced-in
# markdown copy that build_docx_with_diagrams.py regenerates on every run.
data/reports/build/__pycache__/
data/reports/build/*.docx-source.md

# The web client (issue #744): npm's tree, Next's build output, and the
# artefacts Playwright writes on a failing run. `web/` is otherwise fully
# tracked -- it is source, not data.
web/node_modules/
web/.next/
web/out/
web/*.tsbuildinfo
web/test-results/
web/playwright-report/
web/blob-report/
web/*.log
# Next generates this one and rewrites it to point at whichever of `next dev`
# or `next build` ran last, so tracking it means every run dirties the tree
# (#748). `create-next-app` ignores it by default; it was committed by mistake.
web/next-env.d.ts

# Editor settings are personal, not project config.
.vscode/
.idea/

# The per-page census tables under data/reports/names-by-kind/ are row-level
# derived data -- one row per name page, 13 MB over the live corpus, rewritten
# whenever the corpus changes, and a new one appears whenever the corpus mints a
# new name kind. Only the summary tables are tracked; every row-level file
# regenerates from data already on disk in under a minute (build/names_by_kind.py,
# build/answers_census.py).
data/reports/names-by-kind/*
!data/reports/names-by-kind/_index.csv
!data/reports/names-by-kind/questions.csv
!data/reports/names-by-kind/questions-by-source.csv
